Part of Cloudberry's isolation2_schedule on the port's cluster: the
differences from Cloudberry's expected output that have been reviewed, and
are the port's for a reason of its own.

Each file is one test's difference in the form ../../singlenode/canon.pl
gives it, named for the expected output it differs from; a name with a pass
in it (test.orca.diff) holds for that pass alone.  run.sh counts a test as
passing when gpdiff.pl, under Cloudberry's init_file and the port's, finds no
difference or exactly the file's.

concurrent_partition_table_operations_should_not_deadlock, and its
_optimizer output, which the orca pass is compared with
  EXPLAIN's plans and nothing else: the operations the test runs side by
  side do not deadlock.  The planner reads each partition through a Gather
  Motion of its own under an Append, where Cloudberry gathers one Append
  from the segments; ORCA's Dynamic Scan is shown with the scan it runs on
  each partition beneath it.  For the query the index covers, ORCA picks the
  index-only scan Cloudberry's picks, since VACUUM brings back the segments'
  all-visible pages to the coordinator, as Cloudberry's does (vacuum.c).

distributed_snapshot  four things of Cloudberry's that the port does not
  have, and nothing of the distributed snapshots the test is for.
  test_consume_xids is a function of Cloudberry's regress.so, which is not
  built for the port, so the scenario that burns transaction IDs up to a
  segment of the distributed log burns none.  gp_distributed_log is the view
  of that log; the port keeps what it needs of it -- which prepared part
  stands for which distributed transaction -- in shared memory, pruned as
  distributed snapshots advance (gp_internal.dtx_map()), and has no files to
  truncate.  ALTER TABLE ... EXPAND TABLE is M7's, so the rows of a table on
  two of the three segments stay where they are, and the SELECT after it
  shows them there.  And Debug_print_full_dtm is a setting of Cloudberry's,
  whose LOG lines the port does not print.

export_distributed_snapshot
  The distributed fields of an exported snapshot, which the port's file has
  none of: the coordinator's snapshot is the distributed one, sent with each
  statement, so a transaction that imports it reads what its exporter reads
  on every segment, as the test's cases of INSERT, DELETE, UPDATE and DROP
  show.  So the case that corrupts dsxmin corrupts nothing, and SET
  TRANSACTION SNAPSHOT takes the file, where Cloudberry's finds it invalid;
  and a session of the coordinator's own (-1U), an ordinary one here, imports
  the snapshot a distributed session exported, which Cloudberry's utility
  mode refuses.

lockmodes, and its _optimizer output, which the orca pass is compared with
  Four things, none of them a lock of Cloudberry's the port does not take.
  Half the test is append-optimized tables, which are M5's: their CREATE
  fails, and the cases after it with it.  Every lock Cloudberry holds on the
  coordinator is held, as it takes them (O30): without the global deadlock
  detector the ExclusiveLock as the parser opens the table, and with it,
  for a locking clause, AccessShareLock from the parser and then
  RowShareLock or ExclusiveLock -- as Cloudberry's parser holds them, the
  port's taken as the query is planned.  What differs beside them is
  incidental: without the detector Cloudberry's parser looks a table a
  locking clause names up under AccessShareLock first, where the port takes
  the ExclusiveLock alone; the port prints an UPDATE or DELETE for the
  segments, and PostgreSQL's ruleutils takes AccessShareLock on what it
  prints; a cached upsert's EXCLUDED entry asks for RowExclusiveLock,
  after the target's ExclusiveLock; and ORCA, which plans an UPDATE of a
  partitioned table here where Cloudberry's leaves it to the planner, reads
  each partition's constraint, which PostgreSQL builds under an
  AccessShareLock on the table, one lock more beside its ExclusiveLock.  The planner's plans are the port's:
  each table gathered from the segments and joined on the coordinator, and
  with the detector on the rows of a locking clause locked on the segments
  by the gather's query, where Cloudberry's plan has a LockRows.  And
  pg_stats is PostgreSQL 19's, of five columns more, whose tableid the
  port's init_file masks: the root's statistics are Cloudberry's, merged
  from its leaves' as the test's fault waits (gp_partmerge.c), and the
  leaf's correlation is the port's, over the sample as it arrived, where
  Cloudberry's is the mean of each segment's.

segwalrep/commit_blocking_on_standby
  SHOW's column of repl_catchup_within_range, which is the setting's name:
  the port's is gp.repl_catchup_within_range, three characters longer, so
  the header and its rule are too.  The setting is Cloudberry's: 1 WAL
  segment, within which the coordinator waits for a standby that is catching
  up (gp_standby.c), and FTS turns a primary's synchronous replication on
  for a mirror that is (gp_fts.c).

segwalrep/die_commit_pending_replication
  Its one-phase half alone: the port commits in one phase only a segment's
  part that wrote, as it prepares only one that wrote (gp_dtx.c), where
  Cloudberry commits in one phase, on every segment it reached, a
  transaction whose segments wrote nothing (prepareDtxTransaction(),
  cdbtm.c).  So the test's transaction, whose segments only read, reaches
  no segment's one-phase commit, nor the fault the test puts there,
  start_performDtxProtocolCommitOnePhase, and its END commits.  The half the
  test is named for is as Cloudberry's: a segment's backend terminated as
  its COMMIT PREPARED waits for the mirror, the coordinator tells the
  segment again over a new connection, whose COMMIT PREPARED finds the part
  committed and waits for the mirror to have it, as Cloudberry's does
  (gp_dispatch.c, dtx_finish_again()), and the INSERT commits.

segwalrep/fts_unblock_primary
  Where Cloudberry's fault initialize_wal_sender holds the mirror's WAL
  sender: in PostgreSQL 19 no hook runs in a WAL sender after it has
  registered, so the port holds it where the connection is authenticated
  (gp_fts.c), before it is one, and gp_stat_replication shows no state for
  it where Cloudberry's shows startup.  What the test is for holds: FTS
  reports the mirror down while its WAL sender is held, and up and in sync
  once it goes on.

segwalrep/mirror_promotion
  Its tablespace, not its failovers: the port does not dispatch CREATE
  TABLESPACE -- a tablespace is a directory, and the segments of one host
  would share it -- so the table the test puts in one is refused on the
  segments, and its DROP finds nothing.  Both failovers, the one from a
  primary stopped and the one from a primary that does not answer FTS in
  time, and both full recoveries, are as Cloudberry's.

oom_startup_memory  what the memory a process starts with -- 12 MB, which
  each process of a session on a segment takes from the session's and the
  node's limits as it connects (memprot.c) -- does to the test's two
  scenarios, which are the port's dispatch's rather than Cloudberry's.  Its
  join of four gp_dist_random('gp_id') runs on the coordinator, which reads
  each through the session's one connection to each segment, so that no
  segment starts a second process for the session and the session stays
  within the per-query limit, 20 MB, where Cloudberry's four slices start
  four and the second is refused; the 81 rows are three segments' to the
  fourth power.  And the eight sessions' SET of a setting: the port sends a
  setting to the segments with the next statement that goes to them, and a
  SET goes to none, so no session starts a process on a segment and none
  runs past the node's limit, 60 MB, where Cloudberry's SET opens each
  session's processes on the segments and the sixth is refused.  The
  refusals themselves are Cloudberry's, in its words, and the memory
  protection suite makes them happen (../memprot/run.sh).

reader_waits_for_lock, in the planner pass alone
  No reader waits, because the port's planner has none: it reads each
  distributed table of the join through a gather of its own, on the
  segments' writers, and joins on the coordinator, where Cloudberry's
  planner makes a slice for each table, run by readers.  So the writer waits
  for the lock the utility session holds, check_readers_are_blocked() finds
  no reader among the waiters after its two minutes, and the query of them
  finds no row.  Under ORCA the reader and the writer each wait, and the
  test passes.

resource_queue  what pg_stat_activity and pg_locks say of a backend that
  waits for a resource queue's slot: its wait event, which gp_resource
  names ResourceQueue, is an extension's, of type Extension, where
  Cloudberry's has a type of its own; and pg_locks lists no queue.
  Cloudberry's queue is a lock of a lock method of its own, and PostgreSQL
  19's lock methods are fixed, so a slot is held and waited for in
  gp_resource's shared memory (resqueue.c), which
  gp_toolkit.gp_locks_on_resqueue reads.  The waits themselves -- two
  EXECUTEs held until the cursor's transaction ends, then each let through
  -- are Cloudberry's.

runaway_query, in the planner pass alone  EXPLAIN's plan of the query ORCA
  fails on, which the planner makes: the port's planner sends a query whose
  distribution key its condition fixes to the one segment that holds the
  key, and EXPLAIN prints the Gather Motion that reads it there, with the
  relation and the segment, where Cloudberry's shows a Seq Scan and its
  Filter under the Motion.  Under ORCA the fault fails the query, as
  Cloudberry's does.

starve_case
  PostgreSQL's words for the error of function_starve_volatile(): it runs in
  the coordinator's own backend, where Cloudberry runs it in an entry-db
  reader, so the error says where in the function's query it failed -- LINE
  and QUERY -- and names the function with its argument types.  That the
  reader's lock waits behind the waiting AccessExclusiveLock, which is what
  the test is for, holds, each session found waiting by its gp_session_id
  in pg_locks.mppsessionid.

